:Radius rdf:type owl:Class ;
        rdfs:subClassOf :Geometrical_Property .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#ReactorCoolant
:ReactorCoolant rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf :Reactor_Core_Role ;
                <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Reactor Coolant"@en ;
                :R8xx0dhbEh1tjGMDwNZfckV "A substance that is used to remove or transfer heat from the reactor core. Common coolants include light water, heavy water, air, carbon dioxide, helium, liquid sodium, and a sodium-potassium alloy."@en ;
                :referenceARIS <https://aris.iaea.org/Glossary/> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#Reactor_Core
:Reactor_Core rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Whole> ;
              :RR3uD2d7YfhHohfv2Nm6sX "part of a nuclear reactor (3.1.22), where the fissionable nuclides (3.1.2) sustaining the fission chain are located" ;
              :referenceISO <https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/en/#iso:std:iso:12749:-5:ed-1:v1:en:term:3.1.23.1> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#Reactor_Core_Role
:Reactor_Core_Role rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#Role> ;
                   rdfs:label "Reactor Core Role"@en ;
                   :R8xx0dhbEh1tjGMDwNZfckV "A ReactorComponentRole is a role entity defined by its functional relationship with the ReactorCore (the 'Whole'). It does not exist as an isolated entity, but emerges the moment a physical object is integrated into the system, becoming an essential part of the reactor configuration. The existence of the ReactorCore system is ontologically dependent upon the presence of these role entities which, together, enable the controlled fission process." .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#Reactor_Moderator
:Reactor_Moderator rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf :Reactor_Core_Role ;
                   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Reactor Moderator"@en ;
                   :R8xx0dhbEh1tjGMDwNZfckV "A material that is used to decrease the speed of the fast neutron produced during a fission even and thus sustaining a chain reaction."@en ;
                   :referenceARIS "Moderator"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#ResearchReactor
:ResearchReactor rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf :Nuclear_Reactor ;
                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Research Reactor"@en ;
                 :R8xx0dhbEh1tjGMDwNZfckV """type of nuclear reactor (3.1.22) engineered to use its neutron flux and ionizing radiation for scientific research in different fields
Note 1 to entry: The application fields can be medical, biological, electronic, engineering, industrial as well as related to specific reactor concepts or nuclear fuel cycle strategies services, technological developments and/or industrial production purposes. It includes the reactor core (3.1.23.1), the facilities/devices exposed to the neutron field within and around the core, and structures (3.7.1), systems and components (3.7.3) related to the safe operation (3.9.1) of the reactor.
Note 2 to entry: For most of research reactors the heat production from fission power of the nuclear reaction is essentially useless, therefore the denomination “research reactor” becomes a complement regarding “power reactor” (3.2.1.1), for which heat is recovered for further use while the neutron flux and ionizing radiation are not.""" ;
                 :referenceISO <https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/en/#iso:std:iso:12749:-5:ed-1:v1:en:term:3.5.1.2> ;
                 :referenceNSSG <https://vocabulary.iaea.org/iaea-safety-glossary/511.html> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#Rod_Segment
:Rod_Segment rdf:type owl:Class ;
             rdfs:subClassOf :NuclearEnergyObject ,
                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                               owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/emmo/hume#hasPart> ;
                               owl:someValuesFrom :Fuel_Pellet_Column
                             ] ;
             :R8xx0dhbEh1tjGMDwNZfckV "a fuel rod can be subdivided axially into multiple segments. in our definition we assume that each such segment is dedicated to a specific function with a corresponding filling or object. The list of fillings/objects includes: a spring, a metallic spacer/reflector, a stack/column of fuel pellets as isolator/blanket/fuel, a gas plenum, etc." .


